{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2185","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2185","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2185\",\"parent_revision\":\"c57ec676de6b3bccc968aaf0fa491906a1d469830090e7a0bd15943c0e49fe31\",\"root_revision\":\"c57ec676de6b3bccc968aaf0fa491906a1d469830090e7a0bd15943c0e49fe31\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: closed\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **per-rule severity ladder (deny/ask/warn) configurable via destructive-bash-rules.json** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Research entry: ./research/agent-frameworks/claude-code-harness.md \u2014 pattern: R12 (direct push to main/master) defaults to Ask but is configurable per-project as ask/deny/allow. R13 protected file edits emit Warn. The Deny/Ask/Warn ladder lets projects tune severity per environment.\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-05-07\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-05-08)\\n\\n### RT-ICA Assessment\\n\\n**Status: APPROVED-WITH-GAPS**\\n\\n| Input | Status | Source |\\n|-------|--------|--------|\\n| Functional intent | AVAILABLE | Item description \u2014 per-rule severity ladder: deny/ask/warn/allow |\\n| Scope boundaries | AVAILABLE | Sits on top of #2180; single new config file + reader module |\\n| External interfaces | AVAILABLE | Claude Code hook I/O API confirmed \u2014 `permissionDecision: ask` via `hookSpecificOutput` (hooks-io-api SKILL.md lines 290-309) |\\n| Constraints | DERIVABLE | Node.js CJS pattern (matches `block-context-direct-writes.cjs`); project config falls back to default |\\n| Existing system context | AVAILABLE | `hooks/block-context-direct-writes.cjs` lines 122-124 confirm two-state pattern; `hooks.json` structure confirmed |\\n| Verification expectations | AVAILABLE | Measurable signal in item body is concrete and immediately runnable |\\n\\n**Gap**: `#2180` (base guardrail hook) must be implemented before this item can be executed. The config-reader feature has no base hook to apply to yet. This is a sequencing dependency, not a knowledge gap.\\n\\n**Missing inputs that would block execution**: None beyond the dependency on #2180.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: Projects that need environment-specific severity tuning (e.g., allow `rm -rf build/` in CI but warn in interactive sessions)\\n- Bottleneck: Without configurable severity, every project that needs a different posture must fork the hook script; this creates N diverging copies that do not receive upstream fixes\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Projects can tune guardrail severity per environment without forking hook scripts\\n- Warn-level rules create observability without hard blocking, useful for brownfield adoption\\n- Default config ships with the plugin so new projects get sane defaults with zero configuration\\n- Config-reader module is reusable across any future PreToolUse Bash rules added to the harness\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen a PreToolUse Bash hook matches a command against a rule, the hook consults the resolved rule config to determine the action:\\n\\n- `deny` \u2014 exit 2; stderr contains the rule name and reason; Claude Code surfaces this as a blocking error\\n- `ask` \u2014 exit 0; `hookSpecificOutput.permissionDecision` is `\\\"ask\\\"` in stdout JSON; Claude Code surfaces a permission prompt to the user\\n- `warn` \u2014 exit 0; stderr contains a warning message; command proceeds without user interaction\\n- `allow` \u2014 exit 0; no output; command proceeds silently\\n\\nThe project config is optional. When absent, the hook falls back to the default config without printing any error. Config loading is synchronous and completes well within the 5-second hook timeout.\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\nA config-reading module shared across all PreToolUse Bash guardrail hooks. The system has:\\n\\n1. A default config at `plugins/development-harness/hooks/destructive-bash-rules.default.json` \u2014 ships with the plugin, declares all built-in rules with their default severity\\n2. A project override at `<project-root>/.dh/destructive-bash-rules.json` \u2014 optional; keys present here override the default; absent keys fall through to the default\\n3. A config-reader module (e.g., `plugins/development-harness/hooks/load-rules-config.cjs`) \u2014 reads project config, merges with default, returns the resolved rule map\\n4. The hook script `block-destructive-bash.cjs` (from #2180) imports the config-reader and selects the action (`deny | ask | warn | allow`) per matched rule at evaluation time\\n\\nObservable target state:\\n- Project that sets `\\\"rm_rf\\\": {\\\"action\\\": \\\"warn\\\"}` in `.dh/destructive-bash-rules.json` sees `rm -rf build/` exit 0 with warning text on stderr \u2014 command proceeds\\n- Same project with no override for `git push --force` sees it exit 2 (deny) from the default config\\n- A project that removes `.dh/destructive-bash-rules.json` entirely falls back to default config without error\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. Running the measurable signal command from the item (with `warn` config) exits 0 and stderr contains warning text\\n2. Running the same command without the project config override returns the default-config action (exit reflecting `ask` or `deny` per default)\\n3. Running the command against an unrecognised rule ID that is not in either config file exits 0 silently (no crash, no false block)\\n4. `hooks.json` does not require modification \u2014 the config-reader is loaded by the hook script, not wired separately\\n5. `plugins/development-harness/hooks/destructive-bash-rules.default.json` exists and is valid JSON with at least the rules defined in #2180's target state\\n6. `node --check plugins/development-harness/hooks/load-rules-config.cjs` exits 0 (syntax valid)\\n7. The hook exits within the 5-second timeout declared in `hooks.json` even when reading from disk (sync fs read is acceptable for this use case)\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Skill | /plugin-creator:hooks-io-api \u2014 I/O API for hook exit codes and JSON output fields |\\n| Skill | /plugin-creator:hooks-patterns \u2014 CJS hook patterns and permission decision patterns |\\n| Prior work | plugins/development-harness/hooks/block-context-direct-writes.cjs \u2014 existing two-state hook (template for config-reader integration) |\\n| Prior work | plugins/development-harness/hooks/hooks.json \u2014 hook registration structure |\\n| Research | research/insights/2026-05-07-claude-code-harness-improvements.md \u2014 Improvement 6 section (lines 122-142) describes target state verbatim |\\n| Research | research/agent-frameworks/claude-code-harness.md \u2014 R12/R13 severity pattern source |\\n| Backlog | #2180 \u2014 delivers base hook `block-destructive-bash.cjs` that this item extends |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: **#2180** (PreToolUse Bash guardrail rules for destructive commands) \u2014 must be implemented first; this item adds the configurable severity surface on top of the base hook delivered by #2180\\n- Blocks: None identified\\n\\n### Blockers\\n\\n- **#2180 not yet implemented**: `block-destructive-bash.cjs` does not exist at `plugins/development-harness/hooks/block-destructive-bash.cjs`. This item cannot be executed until #2180 delivers that file. Work on #2185 can begin only after #2180 is merged.\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nSmall \u2014 the config-reader module is ~50 lines of synchronous Node.js; JSON merge logic is trivial. The hook integration is a one-function call at the action-dispatch point. The default config file is a straightforward JSON authoring task. Main work is in #2180 (the base hook), which is a prerequisite.\\n\\n## Current State\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`plugins/development-harness/hooks/block-context-direct-writes.cjs` line 122-124 has only one outcome: exit 2 (block) or exit 0 (allow). There is no third state (warn-but-permit, ask-with-default-deny). Neither does the script consult a project-local config to decide its severity \u2014 the rule is hardcoded as deny. Same pattern for any other future PreToolUse Bash rule built off the same template.\\n\\nThis means a project that wants to permit `git push --force` on personal scratch branches but deny it on protected branches has no way to express that without forking the hook script. Harness solves this with a per-rule severity ladder where R12 defaults to Ask but the project can lower it to Allow or raise it to Deny via config.\\n\\nThis proposal sits on top of issue #2180 (PreToolUse Bash guardrail rules for destructive commands) \u2014 once that base set of rules exists, this proposal adds the configurable severity surface.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Target State\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nA config file at `<project-root>/.dh/destructive-bash-rules.json` (and a default at `plugins/development-harness/hooks/destructive-bash-rules.default.json`) with this schema:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\\"rules\\\": {\\n    \\\"git_push_force\\\": {\\\"action\\\": \\\"deny\\\", \\\"patterns\\\": [\\\"git push --force\\\", \\\"git push -f\\\"]},\\n    \\\"rm_rf\\\": {\\\"action\\\": \\\"ask\\\", \\\"patterns\\\": [\\\"rm -rf\\\", \\\"rm -fr\\\"]},\\n    \\\"git_reset_hard_main\\\": {\\\"action\\\": \\\"deny\\\", \\\"patterns\\\": [\\\"git reset --hard main\\\", \\\"git reset --hard master\\\"]},\\n    \\\"no_verify\\\": {\\\"action\\\": \\\"deny\\\", \\\"patterns\\\": [\\\"--no-verify\\\"]}\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nAction semantics:\\n- `deny` \u2014 exit 2 (block, stderr surfaces error)\\n- `ask` \u2014 exit 0 with structured stderr that Claude Code surfaces as a permission request (`{\\\"hookSpecificOutput\\\": {\\\"permissionDecision\\\": \\\"ask\\\"}}`)\\n- `warn` \u2014 exit 0 with stderr printed but command proceeds\\n- `allow` \u2014 exit 0 silent\\n\\nThe hook script reads the project config, falls back to default config when missing, and applies the configured action per rule. Project authors can override severity without editing the hook source.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Measurable Signal\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nCreate `.dh/destructive-bash-rules.json` with `\\\"rm_rf\\\": {\\\"action\\\": \\\"warn\\\"}`. Run: `echo '{\\\"hook_event_name\\\":\\\"PreToolUse\\\",\\\"tool_name\\\":\\\"Bash\\\",\\\"tool_input\\\":{\\\"command\\\":\\\"rm -rf build/\\\"}}' | node plugins/development-harness/hooks/block-destructive-bash.cjs`\\n\\nExpected: exit 0, stderr contains warning text. Same command with default config returns exit code reflecting `ask` action.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"d007e5abc2a73539a7bf2e66d2d86bb94df1d6bf9d7050384a122240ccab848f\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABVCUXOw\"}"}